### What is the Valid Palindrome problem?

The problem asks whether a given string is a palindrome after ignoring all non-alphanumeric characters and treating uppercase and lowercase letters as equal. For example, the string `"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"` is a valid palindrome because after removing punctuation and spaces and lowercasing, it reads `amanaplanacanalpanama`, which is the same forwards and backwards.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Input:  "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
// Cleaned: "amanaplanacanalpanama"
// This reads the same forwards and backwards -> valid palindrome
```

### Why use two pointers instead of cleaning and reversing?

The naive approach strips all non-alphanumeric characters into a new string, reverses it, and compares. This works but allocates O(n) extra space for the cleaned string and O(n) for the reversed copy. The two-pointer approach achieves the same result in O(1) extra space by working directly on the original string.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// NAIVE: O(n) extra space
String cleaned = s.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "").toLowerCase();
String reversed = new StringBuilder(cleaned).reverse().toString();
return cleaned.equals(reversed);

// TWO POINTERS: O(1) extra space
// Work directly on original string, skip non-alphanumeric in place
```

### How do the two pointers work?

You set `left = 0` and `right = s.length() - 1`. The outer loop runs while `left < right`. Inside, you first advance `left` past any character where `Character.isLetterOrDigit` is false, and similarly retreat `right` past non-alphanumeric characters. Once both pointers rest on alphanumeric characters, you compare them after lowercasing. If they differ, it is not a palindrome and you return false. If they match, you move both inward and continue.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PalindromeValidator {

    public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = s.length() - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) {
                left++;
            }
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(right))) {
                right--;
            }
            if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(left))
                    != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(right))) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PalindromeValidator validator = new PalindromeValidator();
        System.out.println(validator.isPalindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"));
        System.out.println(validator.isPalindrome("race a car"));
        System.out.println(validator.isPalindrome(" "));
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
true
```

### Why must the inner skip loops check left < right?

The inner loops that skip non-alphanumeric characters must include the guard `left < right`. Without this guard, on a string that is entirely punctuation or has a long tail of punctuation, the left pointer could advance past the right pointer, causing an out-of-bounds comparison or comparing characters that have already been passed.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: inner loop without guard
while (!Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;
// On input "!!!" this runs off the end of the string

// CORRECT: inner loop with guard
while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;
// On input "!!!" the pointers meet and the outer loop exits cleanly
```

### What are the edge cases?

Several edge cases are important: an empty string is a valid palindrome because the pointers meet immediately, a string of only punctuation is valid for the same reason, a single character is always a palindrome, and strings with mixed digits and letters must compare digits against digits and letters against letters.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: empty string "" -> true (pointers meet immediately)
// Edge case 2: all punctuation ".,!" -> true (pointers skip everything)
// Edge case 3: single char "a" -> true (left == right, loop does not run)
// Edge case 4: "0P" -> false ('0' != 'p', digit vs letter)
// Edge case 5: "Aa" -> true ('a' == 'a' after lowercasing)
```

### How does this compare to using a cleaned StringBuilder?

Both approaches produce the correct answer, but they differ in space usage and allocation cost. The two-pointer approach is preferred in interviews because it demonstrates in-place thinking.

| Approach | Time | Space | Allocations |
|---|---|---|---|
| Two pointers on original | O(n) | O(1) | None |
| Clean + reverse | O(n) | O(n) | New string + reversed copy |
| Clean + two pointers | O(n) | O(n) | New cleaned string |

```java
package com.bank.service;

// Clean + reverse approach (works but wastes space)
public class CleanReverseValidator {
    public boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(c)) {
                sb.append(Character.toLowerCase(c));
            }
        }
        String cleaned = sb.toString();
        return cleaned.equals(sb.reverse().toString());
    }
}
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, printed reference codes contain dashes and spaces for human readability, but a legacy checksum rule requires the alphanumeric core to be a palindrome. Rajesh validates each code during batch processing without allocating extra strings.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ReferenceCodeValidator {

    private final PalindromeValidator palindromeValidator = new PalindromeValidator();

    public boolean isValidChecksum(String referenceCode) {
        if (referenceCode == null || referenceCode.isEmpty()) {
            return true; // empty code is trivially valid
        }
        return palindromeValidator.isPalindrome(referenceCode);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReferenceCodeValidator validator = new ReferenceCodeValidator();

        String code1 = "NBI-121-IBN";
        String code2 = "HDFC-9009-CFHD";
        String code3 = "ICICI-2024";

        System.out.println(code1 + " valid: " + validator.isValidChecksum(code1));
        System.out.println(code2 + " valid: " + validator.isValidChecksum(code2));
        System.out.println(code3 + " valid: " + validator.isValidChecksum(code3));
    }
}
```

```text
NBI-121-IBN valid: true
HDFC-9009-CFHD valid: true
ICICI-2024 valid: false
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the guard on the inner skip loops, which causes index-out-of-bounds on strings that are entirely punctuation. Another mistake is comparing characters without lowercasing, which fails on mixed-case palindromes. A third mistake is using `Character.isAlphabetic` instead of `Character.isLetterOrDigit`, which excludes digits from the comparison.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: using isAlphabetic (excludes digits)
while (left < right && !Character.isAlphabetic(s.charAt(left))) left++;
// Input "121" would skip all digits, treating it as empty

// CORRECT: using isLetterOrDigit (includes digits)
while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) left++;
// Input "121" correctly checks 1-2-1 as a palindrome
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should note that `Character.toLowerCase` handles Unicode correctly, unlike a manual `c - 'A' + 'a'` cast that only works for ASCII. For production code processing international account names, the Unicode-aware methods are essential. The two-pointer palindrome check is also the building block for more advanced problems like longest palindromic substring (expand-around-center) and palindrome partitioning.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Unicode-safe: Character.toLowerCase handles all scripts
// ASCII-only shortcut: (char)(c - 'A' + 'a') fails on non-ASCII
// Time:  O(n) single pass, each character visited at most once
// Space: O(1) no extra allocation
// This is the foundation for expand-around-center palindrome problems
```
